WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.800
The Flash Season 3 finale delivered some twists
and turns, an uncertain fate for our hero,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.809
and a sneaky easter egg that likely reveals
the Big Bad for Season 4.

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:14.259
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and there's just so much to discuss after

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:18.750
that Season 3 finale, so in this video, I'm
going to break down some of the best easter

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.370
eggs from the finale episode, "Finish Line",
and the rest of Season 3, plus I'll be explaining

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.960
the teaser for Season's 4 big villain.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.060
Just before I get stuck in, a quick reminder
that my Arrowverse giveaway is running on

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.060
this video.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.989
And for a chance to win any of these cool
Funko Pops, be sure to subscribe and leave

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:39.640
a comment about the season finale, season
3 easter eggs, or your predictions for Season

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.640
4.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.500
Now, spoiler warning for you if you're not
caught up yet on Season 3, and if you want

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.161
a spoiler-free video check out my 10 awesome
facts you probably didn't know about The Flash

00:00:50.161 --> 00:00:52.100
by tapping up here.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.609
So, let's start with that shocking ending
where Barry sacrifices himself for the good

00:00:56.609 --> 00:00:59.300
of Central City by entering the Speed Force.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.289
The title of the finale, "Finish Line", was
already hinting that Barry would be reaching

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:07.560
an end of sorts and, visually, the shot of
him walking into the Speed Force, which is

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.371
personified as his mother, Nora, feels like
an homage to the final comic book cover of

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:14.500
The Flash volume 1, issue 350.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.030
But this episode is a bittersweet spin on
that comic book story as in that issue the

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.170
Flash is finally reunited with the love of
his life, Iris, whereas in the show, of course,

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.079
he has to leave Iris after spending the whole
season trying to save her.

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:32.700
Just before he leaves, Iris says Central City
needs the Flash, and Barry responds by saying

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.850
they'll have one and embracing Wally.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:40.000
Now, in the comics Wally West took on the
mantle of The Flash after Barry Allen's death,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.909
so is the TV show hinting at that as a likely
future?

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:47.560
I don't think the series would be the same
without Grant Gustin's Flash, and in the 2024

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.679
newspaper headline that we saw in the finale
we still see a picture of Gustin's Flash on

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:55.969
the cover, suggesting we will be getting Grant
Gustin back at some point.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:00.369
The real question, then, is how long will
it be in Season 4 before what's left of Team

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:05.369
Flash venture into the Speed Force with a
plan to break Barry out or before Barry himself

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:06.750
discovers a way to return.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:11.140
An excuse for Team Flash to rescue Barry from
the Speed Force next season, or for him to

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.780
break out himself, would be that they need
his help to defeat the show's new Big Bad.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.560
And in the scene with Savitar and Barry at
Star Labs, there's a clever easter egg for

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.060
who we should expect that baddie to be when
Savitar says to Barry, "This is where we came

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.599
up with the cerebral inhibitor to use against
DeVoe", and then he adds, "you haven't gotten

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:30.599
there yet".

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:34.610
DeVoe was also name-dropped earlier this season
by the villain Abra Kadabra in episode 18,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.230
and these two mentions are a big tease for
our future Flash foe.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.080
In the comics, Clifford DeVoe is a lawyer-turned-criminal
and is the first villain who took on the alias

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.200
of the "Thinker".

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.680
DeVoe makes use of a Thinking Cap device that
gives him mind control and other telekinetic

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.849
abilities.

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:53.930
Given that Flash producer Andrew Kreisberg
already confirmed that, after three seasons

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.840
of speedster villains, the big bad for season
4 would not be a speedster, so DeVoe is a

00:02:58.840 --> 00:02:59.840
likely candidate.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.160
Speaking of villains, there was also a cool
easter egg and tease for another DC Comics

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.379
villain who I wonder if we'll see in a future
season of The Flash.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:11.549
The show's penultimate episode dropped a very
cool Wonder Woman easter egg when Barry and

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:16.879
Captain Cold broke into ARGUS and walked past
a holding cell labelled Cheetah, who's a major

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:18.140
Wonder Woman villain.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.841
There've been several distinct incarnations
of Cheetah over the years in the comics, but

00:03:21.841 --> 00:03:26.780
her first appearance was back in 1943 when
she was co-created by William Moulton Marston,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.760
who, of course, also gave us the character
of Wonder Woman.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.269
By the way if you're a Wonder Woman fan, check
out my video with some of the best Wonder

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:37.810
Woman easter eggs in season 2 of Supergirl
and, I'll also be making some videos soon

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.370
on the new Wonder Woman movie as well.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.480
The title of the episode where we get the
Cheetah tease is "Infantino Street", which

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.810
is an homage to the remarkable comic-book
artist Carmine Infantino, who reinvented the

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.760
Flash in 1956 when he co-created the character
of Barry Allen.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.390
Among the many other characters Infantino
created are Wally West; Solovar, who appeared

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:01.530
in the episode Attack on Gorilla City; and
the villainous Abra Kadabra, who I mentioned

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.530
earlier.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.700
Ok, let's look at some more of the best Season
3 easter eggs.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.189
The titles of the first two episodes of this
season are Flashpoint and Paradox, which is

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:15.360
a sly wink to the animated film, Justice League:
The Flashpoint Paradox, which was an adaptation

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.160
of the 2011 comic book storyline Flashpoint,
which Season 3 of The Flash also draws on.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.190
And the scene where Barry battles The Rival
in the season premiere is a clear call-back

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:30.900
to the show's pilot episode which also featured
tornadoes, Barry racing around them, a Weather

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.030
Wizard name-drop, and Joe West shooting Barry's
adversary.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.850
And the apple obviously hasn't fallen fall
far from the tree in the West family, because

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.860
the way Iris shoots Savitar to save Barry
in this season's finale echoes the way Joe

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.930
repeatedly saves Barry in the same manner.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.220
When criminals Sam Scudder and Rosalind Dillon
are transformed into the meta-humans Mirror

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.070
Master and The Top, there's a lovely hat-tip
to the co-creator of their comic-book counterparts,

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.440
writer John Broome, as the building where
their transformation takes place belongs to

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.530
Broome Industries.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:07.210
And when The Top robs a bank along with her
meta-human partner-in-crime, the colours of

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.180
the outfit she's wearing are a nod to the
green-and-yellow costume worn by the comic-book

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.530
character she's based on, Roscoe Dillon.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:18.500
Plus, Wells says there a Mirror Master on
his earth, who uses a mirror gun and is called

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.360
Evan McCulloch, and that's the same as the
second Mirror Master character who exists

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.430
in the comics.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:29.160
In The Flash's alien invasion crossover episode,
when Team Flash bring together Team Arrow,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.870
Legends, and Supergirl to face the Dominators,
they all gather in a STAR Labs hangar that

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:39.460
looks just like the Hall of Justice, which
was the superheroes' headquarters in the 1970s

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.479
animated TV series, Super Friends.

00:05:41.479 --> 00:05:46.520
And that popular animated show gets another
big shout-out in the Flash and Supergirl musical

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.759
crossover episode where Barry and Kara sing
a duet called Super Friends, which was written

00:05:50.759 --> 00:05:56.270
by Rachel Bloom, the star and creator of another
superb CW show, Crazy Ex-Girlfriend.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:00.990
The song also includes lots of Flash-related
puns, just like in the comics, a nod to how

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:06.460
Barry's messed up the timeline, and some light-hearted
mocking of Kara's cousin, Superman.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:11.050
The prolific and influential comic-book writer
Gardner Fox, who co-created the character

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:16.000
of the Flash in 1940, gets a subtle tribute
on this poster when the Trickster commits

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.810
a robbery on Earth-3 at Fox Bank.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.729
Given that Barry Allen visits Earth 3 to speak
to Jay Garrick in this episode, it's especially

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:29.389
fitting that Gardner Fox receives a name-check
here as Fox not only created the first Flash,

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:35.220
aka Jay Garrick, but also his 1961 story,
'Flash of Two Worlds' brought together Jay

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.100
Garrick and Barry Allen, who inhabited parallel
worlds, and launched the DC Multiverse.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.060
And did you also notice that Fox Bank is located
on Nelson Street?

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.340
Which could be a possible reference to another
of Gardner Fox's creations: Kent Nelson, aka

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.199
Doctor Fate.

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:57.139
When Cisco suits up to battle the Earth-19
bounty hunter Gypsy, and again later Killer

00:06:57.139 --> 00:07:01.430
Frost, the costume he wears is a call-back
on the outfit his comic-book namesake wore

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:07.370
in the New 52 Vibe comics, including its colour
and the quilted textured effect on it.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:12.460
When Barry's memory is accidentally wiped
in episode 21, he can't even remember his

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.660
own name, but when Team Flash tell him he's
called Bartholomew Henry Allen, or Barry for

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:21.400
short, his impulse is for them to call him
Bart instead.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:25.740
Which is a delightful easter egg to the comics
where Bart Allen is a speedster who, at different

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.490
times, takes on the mantle of Kid Flash and
The Flash, and his grandparents just happen

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.199
to be Barry Allen and Iris West!

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:37.430
By the way, this isn't the first time Bart
Allen's been name-checked in the Arrowverse.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:42.820
During the Arrow-Flash crossover in 2014,
Quentin Lance got Barry's name wrong, and

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.419
called him Bart Allen!

